        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(cron): prevent double dispatch after disable/enable cycle during in-flight run",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nDisabling and re-enabling a cron job while one of its runs is still in flight clears the persisted `runningAtMs` reservation, so the next scheduler tick dispatches a second concurrent run. The result is two overlapping executions of a job that is supposed to run once.\n\nThe root cause is that `isRunnableJob` only consulted the persisted `runningAtMs` field and never checked the in-memory active-jobs registry (`isCronJobActive`). When `update(id, { enabled: false })` cleared `runningAtMs` unconditionally (even during an in-flight run), and a subsequent `update(id, { enabled: true })` recomputed `nextRunAtMs` to a past-due time, the next tick found no concurrency guard and dispatched a second run.\n\n## Changes\n\nFour files, 197 insertions, 2 deletions:\n\n1. **`src/cron/service/timer.ts`** (`isRunnableJob`): Added `isCronJobActive(job.id)` check so the in-memory active-jobs registry guards against double dispatch even when `runningAtMs` was cleared externally. This is the primary fix matching the issue's recommendation.\n\n2. **`src/cron/service/ops.ts`** (`finalizeUpdatedJob`): The disable branch now preserves `runningAtMs` when the job is currently in-flight, making the guard durable across process restarts (defense-in-depth).\n\n3. **`src/cron/service/jobs.ts`** (`normalizeJobTickState`): The disabled-job branch now uses the same `STUCK_RUN_MS` (2h) gate as enabled jobs instead of unconditionally clearing `runningAtMs`. This prevents the maintenance recompute path from undoing fix 2 and keeps behavior consistent.\n\n4. **`src/cron/service.issue-102238-double-dispatch.test.ts`** (new): Committed regression test covering the exact disable→re-enable→tick sequence end to end with real persisted store, real scheduler code paths, and diagnostic state dumps at every step.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**Unit tests (124/124 passed, 1308/1308 tests):**\n```\n Test Files  124 passed (124)\n      Tests  1308 passed (1308)\n```\n\n**Committed regression test (+1 file, +1 test):**\n```\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  1 passed (1)\n```\n\n**Real behavior proof — live terminal transcript driving the real cron scheduler runtime end to end:**\n\n```\n==== REAL BEHAVIOR PROOF: #102238 ====\nhostname: LIN-66D8BD4EA2C\npid: 3484927\ntimestamp: 2026-07-08T17:07:14Z\n\n--- while first run in flight (after manual force run) ---\ndispatches=1\nrunningAtMs=1783598400000\nnextRunAtMs=1783598340000\nactiveRegistrySaysRunning=true\n\n--- after update disable then update enable (still mid-run) ---\nrunningAtMs=1783598400000           ← FIX 2: preserved!\nnextRunAtMs=1783598340000\nnextRunAtMsIsPastDue=true\nactiveRegistrySaysRunning=true\n\n--- after one scheduler tick (onTimer) ---\ndispatches=1                        ← FIX: NO second dispatch!\nmaxConcurrentInFlight=1\n\n--- after original run completes ---\nrunningAtMs=undefined\nlastRunAtMs=1783598400000\nlastStatus=ok\nmaxConcurrentInFlight=1\n```\n\nThe proof drives the real scheduler code paths end to end via `ops.run(id, \"force\")` → `ops.update(id, { enabled: false })` → `ops.update(id, { enabled: true })` → `timer.onTimer(state)` against a real persisted JSON store. The `runIsolatedAgentJob` execution boundary is the only stub — the same stubbing used in the upstream issue's own proof. Raw state is read directly from disk (`loadCronStore`) at each step, and `isCronJobActive()` reflects the process-global active-jobs registry.\n\nKey findings from the transcript:\n- `runningAtMs=1783598400000` is preserved after disable/enable (Fix 2)\n- `activeRegistrySaysRunning=true` throughout (Fix 1)\n- `dispatches=1` after the scheduler tick — no second dispatch\n- `maxConcurrentInFlight=1` — exactly one concurrent run, as expected\n\n**Lint + Type check:**\nNo new lint or type errors introduced.\n\n## Review\n- Manually reviewed and verified\n- AI-assisted: Yes\n- Fixes #102238",
